  "knowledgePattern": "Eliminate the coefficient using two integer roots, then exhaust the small factor cases",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Name the two roots",
      "body": "<p>Call the positive integer roots <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>. Substitute each into the cubic, then subtract the two equations to eliminate the constant.</p>",
      "recap": "A root makes the polynomial equal to zero.",
      "pitfall": "The two roots are distinct, so r minus s is nonzero.",
      "pause": "Factor the difference of cubes and express a using r and s.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b4-l02",
          "reminder": "A root makes the polynomial equal to zero."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Remove the coefficient",
      "body": "<p>Substitute your expression for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> back into one of the root equations. Factor what remains so that only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> appear.</p>",
      "recap": "Substitution can eliminate an unknown coefficient.",
      "pitfall": "Keep the minus signs from your expression for a.",
      "pause": "Find the integer equation for r and s.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l01",
          "reminder": "Substitution can eliminate an unknown coefficient."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Bound the smaller root",
      "body": "<p>Use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in your factored equation to bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>. Test each possible integer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>, then check the original cubic.</p>",
      "recap": "A short list of cases is complete only after a bound excludes the others.",
      "pitfall": "A trial pair must satisfy the whole factored equation.",
      "pause": "Find the roots, recover a and verify the other root.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b1-l09",
          "reminder": "A short list of cases is complete only after a bound excludes the others."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Let the distinct positive integer roots be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>. Subtracting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>120</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>120</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and dividing by nonzero <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>, gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Substitution into either root equation gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>120</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>, we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>120</mn></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. This bounds the possible integer search.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>120</mn></mrow></math>, impossible because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>120</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>61</mn></mrow></math>, not an integer square. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> as the positive root.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>25</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>49</mn></mrow></math>, answer E. Finally <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>49</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>120</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> confirms exactly the two positive integer roots and the remaining negative root.</p>"
